Debounce is subtly broken; prove it with fake timers. Set up /workspace with vitest (npm i -D vitest) and save src/debounce.ts:

export function debounce<A>(fn: (arg: A) => void, waitMs: number) {
  let timer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null = null;
  let pendingArg: A;
  return (arg: A) => {
    if (timer === null) {
      pendingArg = arg;
      timer = setTimeout(() => {
        fn(pendingArg);
        timer = null;
      }, waitMs);
    }
  };
}

This is not a debounce: it captures the FIRST call's arguments and drops later calls instead of resetting the timer (it is a broken throttle). Write src/debounce.test.ts using vi.useFakeTimers(): call debounced('a'), advance 50ms, call debounced('b'), advance 100ms past the wait, and assert fn was called once with 'b' exactly waitMs after the LAST call; the current code calls it with 'a' at the wrong time, so the test must fail first. Run npx vitest run, confirm, then fix debounce properly (clearTimeout and reschedule on every call, invoke with the latest args). Add tests: rapid burst of 10 calls fires once; two bursts separated by more than waitMs fire twice; timers cleaned up (vi.getTimerCount() back to 0 after firing). All green with fake timers only, no real waiting.

